(4625744, 664975, 20608, '<td valign="top">Do not worry Adam, we know you ROCK DUDE!</td>', 'Do not worry Adam, we know you ROCK DUDE!', '2008-04-09 03:56:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(4625747, 664257, 30982, '<td valign="top">A nice, quiet and peaceful shot.  For the challenge, I think your commenters hit on it - not enough warm colors to carry it.  I think this is a very good example of how the colors compliment each other to provide the total picture - warms and cools balanced together.  Your foreground, for example is very cool in color tone - yet the reds/golds/yellows in the sky add a warmth to the shot.  Make sense?</td>', 'A nice, quiet and peaceful shot.  For the challenge, I think your commenters hit on it - not enough warm colors to carry it.  I think this is a very good example of how the colors compliment each other to provide the total picture - warms and cools balanced together.  Your foreground, for example is very cool in color tone - yet the reds/golds/yellows in the sky add a warmth to the shot.  Make sense?', '2008-04-09 04:01:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(4625751, 663151, 30982, '<td valign="top">I see this as warm, both in tone and feeling.  Neutrals - beiges, browns, tans - are often hard to use to convey "color" since, well, many see them as neutral.  Are you shooting in RAW and do you use CS3?  The reason I ask is there''s a very handy tool in the CS3 RAW processor (and it may well exist in Nikon software - I don''t know) that allows you to "recover" the highlights.</td>', 'I see this as warm, both in tone and feeling.  Neutrals - beiges, browns, tans - are often hard to use to convey "color" since, well, many see them as neutral.  Are you shooting in RAW and do you use CS3?  The reason I ask is there''s a very handy tool in the CS3 RAW processor (and it may well exist in Nikon software - I don''t know) that allows you to "recover" the highlights.', '2008-04-09 04:04:31.000000', NULL, 0),
